Looking at this problem, I need to determine whether every continuous automorphism of $\mathbb{C}_p$ arises as a limit-extension of some $\bar{\sigma} \in \text{Gal}(\bar{\mathbb{Q}}_p/\mathbb{Q}_p)$.

**Answer Leak Self-Check:** The problem text contains no leaked solutions, answer values, solution sketches, or formalization notes. Proceeding to solve.

---

## Proof

**Claim:** Yes. Every continuous (field) automorphism $\sigma$ of $\mathbb{C}_p$ can be expressed as
$$\sigma(x) = \lim_{n\to\infty} \bar{\sigma}(x_n), \quad x = \lim_{n\to\infty} x_n,\ x_n \in \bar{\mathbb{Q}}_p,$$
for a unique $\bar{\sigma} \in \text{Gal}(\bar{\mathbb{Q}}_p/\mathbb{Q}_p)$.

---

### Step 1: $\sigma$ fixes $\mathbb{Q}_p$.

Any field automorphism $\sigma$ of $\mathbb{C}_p$ fixes the prime field $\mathbb{Q}$ pointwise. Since $\sigma$ is **continuous** (with respect to the $p$-adic topology) and $\mathbb{Q}$ is dense in $\mathbb{Q}_p$ (the completion of $\mathbb{Q}$ at $p$), for any $q \in \mathbb{Q}_p$, pick a sequence $q_n \in \mathbb{Q}$ with $q_n \to q$. Then
$$\sigma(q) = \sigma\!\left(\lim q_n\right) = \lim \sigma(q_n) = \lim q_n = q.$$
Hence $\sigma|_{\mathbb{Q}_p} = \mathrm{id}_{\mathbb{Q}_p}$. Consequently $\sigma^{-1}$ also fixes $\mathbb{Q}_p$ pointwise.

---

### Step 2: $\sigma$ restricts to an automorphism $\bar{\sigma}$ of $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}_p$ fixing $\mathbb{Q}_p$.

**($\sigma$ maps $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}_p$ into itself.)** Let $\alpha \in \bar{\mathbb{Q}}_p$, so $\alpha$ is algebraic over $\mathbb{Q}_p$ with minimal polynomial $f(t) \in \mathbb{Q}_p[t]$. Applying $\sigma$ to $f(\alpha)=0$ and using $\sigma|_{\mathbb{Q}_p}=\mathrm{id}$:
$$f(\sigma(\alpha)) = \sigma(f(\alpha)) = 0.$$
Thus $\sigma(\alpha)$ is a root of $f$, hence algebraic over $\mathbb{Q}_p$, so $\sigma(\alpha) \in \bar{\mathbb{Q}}_p$.

**($\sigma|_{\bar{\mathbb{Q}}_p}$ is surjective onto $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}_p$.)** Let $\beta \in \bar{\mathbb{Q}}_p$ with minimal polynomial $g(t) \in \mathbb{Q}_p[t]$. Since $\sigma$ is surjective on $\mathbb{C}_p$, there exists $x \in \mathbb{C}_p$ with $\sigma(x)=\beta$, i.e., $x = \sigma^{-1}(\beta)$. Applying $\sigma^{-1}$ to $g(\beta)=0$ and using $\sigma^{-1}|_{\mathbb{Q}_p}=\mathrm{id}$:
$$g(x) = g(\sigma^{-1}(\beta)) = \sigma^{-1}(g(\beta)) = 0.$$
So $x$ is a root of $g$, hence $x \in \bar{\mathbb{Q}}_p$. Therefore $\sigma|_{\bar{\mathbb{Q}}_p}$ is surjective.

Being an injective (as a restriction of an injective map) and surjective field homomorphism, $\bar{\sigma} := \sigma|_{\bar{\mathbb{Q}}_p}$ is a field automorphism of $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}_p$ fixing $\mathbb{Q}_p$, i.e.,
$$\bar{\sigma} \in \text{Gal}(\bar{\mathbb{Q}}_p/\mathbb{Q}_p).$$

---

### Step 3: The limit formula holds.

Since $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}_p$ is dense in $\mathbb{C}_p = \widehat{\bar{\mathbb{Q}}_p}$, every $x \in \mathbb{C}_p$ admits a sequence $(x_n) \subset \bar{\mathbb{Q}}_p$ with $x_n \to x$. By continuity of $\sigma$ and the fact that $\sigma|_{\bar{\mathbb{Q}}_p} = \bar{\sigma}$:
$$\sigma(x) = \sigma\!\left(\lim_{n\to\infty} x_n\right) = \lim_{n\to\infty} \sigma(x_n) = \lim_{n\to\infty} \bar{\sigma}(x_n).$$

This is precisely the required representation.

---

### Step 4: Uniqueness (and converse).

**Uniqueness of $\bar{\sigma}$:** If two elements $\bar{\sigma}_1, \bar{\sigma}_2 \in \text{Gal}(\bar{\mathbb{Q}}_p/\mathbb{Q}_p)$ produce the same continuous automorphism via the limit formula, then $\bar{\sigma}_1$ and $\bar{\sigma}_2$ agree on the dense subset $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}_p$, hence agree everywhere by continuity of the resulting maps. In particular $\bar{\sigma}_1 = \bar{\sigma}_2$ on $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}_p$.

**Converse (every $\bar{\sigma}$ gives a continuous automorphism):** Given $\bar{\sigma} \in \text{Gal}(\bar{\mathbb{Q}}_p/\mathbb{Q}_p)$, note that $\bar{\sigma}$ is automatically **isometric**: for $\alpha \in \bar{\mathbb{Q}}_p$, $|\alpha|_p = |f(\alpha)|_p^{1/\deg}$ considerations show $|\bar{\sigma}(\alpha)|_p = |\alpha|_p$ (since $\bar{\sigma}(\alpha)$ is a conjugate root of the same minimal polynomial, and all roots of an irreducible polynomial over $\mathbb{Q}_p$ have the same $p$-adic absolute value). Being an isometry, $\bar{\sigma}$ is uniformly continuous and extends uniquely to a continuous automorphism of the completion $\mathbb{C}_p$. This extension is exactly the limit formula above, and it is surjective because the extension of an isometric automorphism of a dense subfield to the completion is an isometric automorphism of the completion.

---

### Conclusion

The map
$$\left\{\text{continuous automorphisms of } \mathbb{C}_p\right\} \longrightarrow \text{Gal}(\bar{\mathbb{Q}}_p/\mathbb{Q}_p), \quad \sigma \mapsto \sigma|_{\bar{\mathbb{Q}}_p}$$
is a **bijection**, with inverse given by continuous (isometric) extension via the limit formula
$$\bar{\sigma} \;\mapsto\; \left(x = \lim x_n \;\mapsto\; \lim \bar{\sigma}(x_n)\right).$$

Therefore **every** continuous automorphism of $\mathbb{C}_p$ can be expressed in the stated form, and conversely every element of $\text{Gal}(\bar{\mathbb{Q}}_p/\mathbb{Q}_p)$ gives rise to exactly one such continuous automorphism. $\blacksquare$

